           "The Blessed Lord said: In this world a twofold discipline was taught by me of old, sinless one: the yoga of knowledge for the Sāṅkhyas, and the yoga of action for the yogins.",
           bhashya=[
               {"text": "praśnānurūpameva prativacanaṃ śrī bhagavānuvāca –", "intro": True, "tr": "The Blessed Lord gave a reply that fitted the question:"},
               {"text": "loke'sminniti ḥ– loke asmin śāstrārthānuṣṭhānādhikṛtānāṃ traivarṇikānāṃ, dvividhā dviprakārā, niṣṭhā sthitiḥ anuṣṭheyatātparyam, purā pūrvaṃ sargādau prajāḥ sṛṣṭvā tāsām abhyudayaniḥśreyasaprāptisādhanaṃ vedārthasampradāyam āviṣkurvatā, proktā, mayā sarvajñena īśvareṇa, he anagha apāpa. tatra kā sā dvividhāniṣṭhā ityāha – tatra jñānayogena jñānam eva yogaḥ tena, sāṅkhyānām ātmānātma viṣayavivekajñānavatāṃ, brahmacaryāśramāt eva kṛtasannyāsānāṃ vedāntavijñānasuniścitārthānāṃ paramahaṃsaparivrājakānāṃ brahmaṇyeva avasthitānāṃ, niṣṭhā proktā. karmayogena – karmaiva yogaḥ karmayogaḥ tena karmayogena, yogināṃ karmiṇāṃ niṣṭhā proktā ityarthaḥ.", "tr": "'In this world a twofold discipline…' In this world, for those of the three classes qualified to practise the teaching of scripture, a twofold steadfastness — standing, the purport of what is to be practised — was taught of old, in the beginning of creation, when, having created beings, I set forth the tradition of the Veda's meaning, the means of attaining prosperity and the highest good, by me, the omniscient Lord, sinless one. What is that twofold steadfastness? He says: by the yoga of knowledge — knowledge itself is the yoga — steadfastness is taught for the Sāṅkhyas: those who have discriminating knowledge of Self and non-self, who have renounced from the stage of studentship itself, whose understanding of the meaning of Vedānta is certain, the paramahaṃsa wandering ascetics established in Brahman. By the yoga of action — action itself is the yoga — steadfastness is taught for the yogins, the men of action: that is the meaning."},
               {"text": "yadi ca ekena puruṣeṇa ekasmai puruṣārthāya jñānaṃ karma ca samuccitya anuṣṭheyaṃ bhagavatā iṣṭam, uktaṃ vakṣyamāṇaṃ vā gītāsu, vedeṣu ca uktaṃ katham iha arjunāya upapannāya priyāya viśiṣṭabhinnapuruṣakartṛke eva jñānakarmaniṣṭhe brūyāt? yadi punaḥ “arjunaḥ jñānaṃ karma ca dvayaṃ śrutvā svayameva anuṣṭhāsyati, anyeṣāṃ tu bhinnapuruṣānuṣṭheyatāṃ vakṣyāmi” iti mataṃ bhagavataḥ kalpyeta, tadā rāgadveṣavān, apramāṇabhūtaḥ bhagavān kalpitaḥ syāt. tacca ayuktam. tasmāt kayāpi yuktyā na samuccayaḥ jñānakarmaṇoḥ.", "tr": "If the Lord had wished knowledge and action to be practised together by one person for one human goal, and had said so or would say so in the Gītā, and if it were said so in the Vedas, how could he here tell Arjuna, who had come to him and was dear to him, of steadfastnesses in knowledge and action that are to be practised by distinct, different people? And if it were imagined that the Lord thought, 'Arjuna, having heard of both knowledge and action, will practise both himself; to others I will say they are to be practised by different people', then the Lord would be imagined to have attachment and aversion and to be unreliable — which is not right. Therefore knowledge and action cannot be combined by any reasoning."},
           ],
