               {"text": "avidvānkathaṃ ajñaḥ karmasu sajjata ityāha –", "intro": True, "tr": "How does the ignorant, unwise man become attached to actions? He says:"},
               {"text": "prakṛteḥ iti :– prakṛtiḥ pradhānaṃ sattvarajastamasāṃ guṇānāṃ sāmyāvasthā, tasyāḥ prakṛteḥ guṇaiḥ vikāraiḥ kāryakaraṇarūpaiḥ kriyamāṇāni karmāṇi, laukikāni śāstrīyāṇi ca, sarvaśaḥ sarvaprakāraiḥ ahaṅkāravimūḍhātmā, kāryakaraṇasaṅghātātmapratyayaḥ ahaṅkāraḥ tena vividhaṃ nānāvidhaṃ, mūḍhaḥ ātmā antaḥkaraṇaṃ yasya saḥ ayaṃ kāryakaraṇadharmā, kāryakaraṇābhi'mānī, avidyayā karmāṇi ātmani manyamānaḥ, tattatkarmaṇām ahaṃ kartā iti manyate.", "tr": "'All actions are performed by the guṇas of nature…' Nature is pradhāna, the state of balance of the guṇas sattva, rajas and tamas. Actions, worldly and scriptural, are performed in every way by the guṇas of that nature — by its modifications, the body and senses. One whose self, whose inner organ, is deluded in many ways by egoism — the notion that the aggregate of body and senses is the Self — who takes on the attributes of body and senses and identifies with them, attributing actions to the Self through ignorance, thinks 'I am the doer' of each of those actions."},
