           "Attraction and aversion are fixed in each sense towards its objects. Let no one come under their sway, for they are his adversaries.",
           bhashya=[
               {"text": "yadi sarvaḥ jantuḥ ātmanaḥ prakṛtisadṛśameva ceṣṭate – na ca prakṛtiśūnyaḥ kaścidasti – tataḥ puruṣakārasya viṣayānupapatteḥ śāstrānarthakyaprāptau idam ucyate –", "intro": True, "tr": "If every creature acts only in conformity with its own nature — and no one is without a nature — then human effort has no scope, and scripture would be pointless. To this it is said:"},
               {"text": "indriyasyeti : – indriyasya arthe sarvendriyāṇām arthe śabdādiviṣaye, iṣṭe rāgaḥ aniṣṭe dveṣaḥ ityevaṃ pratīndriyārthaṃ rāgadveṣau avaśyambhāvinau, tatra ayaṃ puruṣakārasya śāstrārthasya ca viṣayaḥ ucyate, śāstrārthe pravṛttaḥ pūrvameva rāgadveṣayoḥ vaśaṃ na āgacchet. yā hi puruṣasya prakṛtiḥ sā rāgadveṣapuraḥsaraiva. svakārye puruṣaṃ pravartayati. tadā svadharmaparityāgaḥ paradharmānuṣṭhānaṃ ca bhavati. yadā punaḥ rāgadveṣau tatpratipakṣeṇa niyamayati tadā śāstradṛṣṭiḥ eva puruṣaḥ bhavati na prakṛtivaśaḥ. tasmāt tayoḥ rāgadveṣayoḥ vaśaṃ na āgacchet. yataḥ tau hi asya puruṣasya paripanthinau śreyomārgasya vighnakartārau taskarau iva pathi ityarthaḥ.", "tr": "'Attraction and aversion are fixed in each sense towards its objects…' In the object of each sense, in sound and the other objects of all the senses, attraction to the desired and aversion to the undesired are inevitable for each sense-object. Here the scope of human effort and of the scripture's teaching is stated: one who engages in the teaching of scripture should from the very start not come under the sway of attraction and aversion. For a man's nature, led by attraction and aversion, drives him to its own work; then come the abandonment of one's own dharma and the practice of another's. But when he restrains attraction and aversion by their opposites, then the man is guided by scripture, not under the sway of nature. Therefore let him not come under the sway of these two, attraction and aversion; for they are his adversaries, obstructing the path to the highest good like robbers on the road — that is the meaning."},
           ],
